  "answer_markdown": "The Mistral toolkit ships with the following text splitters:\n\n1. **Character Text Splitter** – Splits text by character count, ideal for simple text and quick prototyping [1].\n   - Example usage:\n     ```python\n     from mistralai.search.toolkit.ingestion.text_splitters import CharacterTextSplitter\n     \n     splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=500)\n     chunks = splitter.split_document(document)\n     ```\n   - Default `chunk_size` is **1000 characters** [1].\n\n2. **Token Text Splitter** – Splits text token-aware, useful for LLM context management [1].\n   - Example usage:\n     ```python\n     from mistralai.search.toolkit.ingestion.text_splitters import TokenTextSplitter\n     \n     splitter = TokenTextSplitter(chunk_size=1000, chunk_overlap=200)\n     chunks = splitter.split_document(document)\n     ```\n   - Default `chunk_size` is **1000 tokens** (Mistral tokenizer) [1].\n\n3. **Markdown Text Splitter** – Splits Markdown documents at header boundaries (requires `text-splitter-langchain` extra) [1].\n   - Example usage:\n     ```python\n     from mistralai.search.toolkit.ingestion.text_splitters import MarkdownTextSplitter, MarkdownTextSplitterConfig\n     \n     config = MarkdownTextSplitterConfig(\n         headers_to_split_on=[(\"#\", \"h1\"), (\"##\", \"h2\")],\n         chunk_size=1000,\n         chunk_overlap=200\n     )\n     splitter = MarkdownTextSplitter(config=config)\n     chunks = splitter.split_document(document)\n     ```\n\n4. **Separator Text Splitter** – Splits text recursively using custom separators (e.g., paragraphs, sentences) [1].\n   - Example usage:\n     ```python\n     from mistralai.search.toolkit.ingestion.text_splitters import SeparatorTextSplitter, SeparatorTextSplitterConfig\n     \n     config = SeparatorTextSplitterConfig(\n         chunk_size=1000,\n         chunk_overlap=200,\n         chunk_separators=[\"\\n\\n\", \"\\n\", \". \", \" \", \"\"]\n     )\n     splitter = SeparatorTextSplitter(config=config)\n     chunks = splitter.split_document(document)\n     ```\n\n5. **Custom Splitters** – Can be implemented by adhering to the `TextSplitter` protocol [1].\n\n### **How to Pick a Chunk Size**\nThe optimal chunk size depends on your LLM's context window and use case. Mistral recommends:\n- **4k tokens**: 300–500 characters (50–100 overlap) [1].\n- **8k tokens**: 500–1000 characters (100–200 overlap) [1].\n- **32k+ tokens**: 1000–2000 characters (200–500 overlap) [1].\n- **Code/technical**: 500–1000 characters (100–200 overlap) [1].\n- **Legal/financial**: 1000–2000 characters (200–500 overlap) [1].\n\n**Rule of thumb**: ~400–600 characters ≈ 100–150 tokens [1]. Adjust based on domain complexity and retrieval precision needs.\n\nOverlap improves semantic coherence but increases index size and query latency [1].",
